{0 Outputs and lifecycle}

{1 Drain acceptance}

{!type:Observe.Drain.t} is the portable immediate output seam:

{[
let drain =
  Observe.Drain.create (fun log ->
    enqueue_owned log;
    Observe.Drain.Accepted)
]}

The callback receives one immutable globally safe completed log. It must return
promptly and be concurrency-safe. [Accepted] means only that this drain took
immediate ownership; it does not claim formatting, queueing, persistence,
durability, or acknowledgement. Exceptions are contained for that branch.

An immediate drain needs no worker or lifecycle registration. A custom
asynchronous output owns its own finite queue, worker, loss accounting, and
resource cleanup behind this acceptance seam.

{1 Ready console}

[Observe_lwt_unix.init] installs automatic standard-error delivery unless
{!type:Observe.Config.console} is [Silent]. [Auto] chooses pretty development
output or NDJSON production output. Submission is prompt and a finite private
worker serializes writes.

Console writes continue partial writes and retry only interrupted system calls.
There is no fictional cross-destination retry policy.

{1 Ready daily files}

Install [observe-fs-lwt-unix] and create one drain before initialization:

{[
let* files =
  Observe_fs_lwt_unix.create_exn ~dir:".observe/logs" ~capacity:1024 ()
in
let config =
  Observe.Config.create_exn ~service:"orders" ~drains:[ files ] ()
in
Observe_lwt_unix.init_exn config
]}

The ready package recursively prepares the directory, starts one finite worker,
registers it with the process lifecycle, and returns its drain directly. Point
and wide logs append as flat NDJSON to daily UTC [.jsonl] files.

The queue rejects the newest record when full. Once accepted, older append work
is not silently evicted to admit newer records. Writes continue partial writes
and interrupted syscalls; other failures stop that output, report bounded loss,
and do not break application control flow or another output.

Local write completion does not imply [fsync], crash durability, compression,
retention, or cross-process coordination.

{1 Flush and shutdown}

{[
let* () = Observe_lwt_unix.flush () in
continue_running ()
]}

[flush] settles work accepted before its boundary and leaves logging open.

{[
let () =
  Observe_lwt_unix.init_exn config;
  Lwt_main.run (Lwt.finalize app Observe_lwt_unix.shutdown)
]}

[shutdown] is terminal and idempotent. It closes production admission, attempts
accepted work, and releases registered output workers and resources. Ordinary
calls use a finite 30-second caller wait and raise
[Observe_lwt_unix.Lifecycle.Incomplete] when a participant reports
rejection, loss, failure, timeout, or cancellation.

The advanced surface returns the report instead:

{[
let within =
  Observe_lwt_unix.Lifecycle.Duration.create_exn ~seconds:5.
in
let* report = Observe_lwt_unix.Lifecycle.shutdown ~within () in
if not (Observe_lwt_unix.Lifecycle.complete report) then
  inspect (Observe_lwt_unix.Lifecycle.problems report)
]}

A timeout or caller cancellation stops only that caller's finite wait; shared
work continues. Independent flushes may overlap. Concurrent and repeated
shutdown callers join the same transition.

The problem identities are bounded runtime labels. A complete report means the
registered participants settled without a reported problem at their owned
effect boundaries. It still does not upgrade local delivery into durability or
remote acknowledgement.
